# Useful Troubleshooting Commands

## Check Wazuh Services

```bash
sudo systemctl status wazuh-manager
sudo systemctl status wazuh-indexer
sudo systemctl status wazuh-dashboard
sudo systemctl status filebeat
```

---

## View Manager Logs

```bash
sudo tail -f /var/ossec/logs/ossec.log
```

---

## Check Ubuntu IP Address

```bash
hostname -I
```

---

## Verify Windows Agent Service

```powershell
Get-Service WazuhSvc
```

---

## Start Windows Agent

```cmd
NET START WazuhSvc
```

---

## Check APT Processes

```bash
ps -ef | grep -E "apt|dpkg|unattended"
```

---

# Lessons Learned

Throughout this deployment, I learned several practical lessons that are useful when building a SIEM lab.

- Ubuntu automatic updates can interrupt long package installations.
- Verifying service status immediately after installation helps identify incomplete deployments.
- Bridged networking is better suited than NAT for endpoint communication in a virtual lab.
- DHCP-assigned IP addresses can change after a reboot, causing agents to disconnect.
- Checking logs and service status is often more effective than immediately reinstalling software.

Most of the issues I encountered were configuration-related rather than software failures. Understanding the underlying cause made troubleshooting much easier and improved my understanding of how the different Wazuh components communicate with each other.
